gm-method: a shared occlusion-query pipeline with a fixed per-frame query budget much smaller than typical candidate count will produce a hide-rate that scales with candidate count / budget-exhaustion ratio, not a fixed percentage. An anomaly-detection guard tuned as a fixed threshold (e.g. "never legitimately occludes >30% at once") will keep failing at different poses because the true noise floor is state-dependent -- the fix is a stale-verdict fail-open keyed on frames-since-last-fresh-resolve, not another threshold retune. If a guard has already been retuned twice and each time the measured rate crept just under the new threshold, that pattern itself is the diagnostic signal to stop retuning and add an expiry-based fail-open instead.